Guided Exercise: Navigate the OpenShift Web Console
Objective
In this exercise you learn to:
1.	Access the OpenShift Web Console
2.	Create a project
3.	Deploy an application from Git
4.	View pods, deployments, services, and routes
5.	Explore cluster resources using Administrator perspective
This exercise helps you understand how applications run inside OpenShift.
________________________________________
Step 1: Prepare the Lab Environment
Command:
lab start intro-navigate
What this command does
It prepares the environment by:
•	validating the OpenShift cluster
•	resetting the lab state
•	ensuring required services are running
This ensures the lab works correctly.
________________________________________
Step 2: Login to OpenShift Cluster
Command:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
login	authenticate to cluster
-u developer	username
-p developer	password
https://api...	API server address
If login is successful:
Login successful
Now the CLI can interact with the cluster.
________________________________________
Step 3: Get Web Console URL
Command:
oc whoami --show-console
Example output:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
What it does
This command prints the web console address.
You open this URL in a browser to access the OpenShift GUI.
________________________________________
Step 4: Login to Web Console
Steps:
1.	Open browser
2.	Enter console URL
3.	Select Red Hat Identity Management
4.	Login using:
username: developer
password: developer
You will reach the OpenShift Home Page.
If a tour appears → click Skip Tour.
________________________________________
Step 5: Use Developer Perspective
Developers use the Developer Perspective to deploy applications.
Menu path:
+Add → Create Project
________________________________________
Step 6: Create a New Project
Create project:
Name: intro-navigate
Display Name: intro-navigate
Description: sample project
Then click:
Create
What is a Project?
A Project = Kubernetes Namespace + OpenShift features
Projects allow:
•	resource isolation
•	multi-user environments
•	application grouping
CLI equivalent:
oc new-project intro-navigate
________________________________________
Step 7: View Topology Page
After creating the project:
Topology
This page visually shows:
•	applications
•	pods
•	services
•	connections
It is the graphical representation of the application architecture.
________________________________________
Step 8: Deploy Application from Git
Go to:
+Add → Import from Git
Enter:
https://git.ocp4.example.com/developer/intro-navigate.git
Git Type:
GitLab
Then click:
Create
________________________________________
What Happens After Clicking Create
OpenShift automatically performs several actions.
Pipeline:
Git repository
       ↓
Build container image
       ↓
Create deployment
       ↓
Create pod
       ↓
Expose service
So developers only provide source code.
OpenShift handles the rest.
________________________________________
Step 9: Application Appears in Topology
The Topology page now shows:
intro-navigate-git-app
This represents the deployed application.
Inside this application:
•	Deployment
•	Pod
•	Service
________________________________________
Step 10: View Deployment Details
Click the D icon.
This opens the deployment panel.
Select:
Details
Wait until:
1 Pod Running
This means the application has successfully started.
________________________________________
Deployment Concept
A Deployment manages pods.
Responsibilities:
•	create pods
•	update pods
•	scale pods
CLI command:
oc get deployments
________________________________________
Step 11: Deployment Actions
Click:
Actions
Available actions include:
•	restart deployment
•	scale pods
•	delete deployment
•	edit configuration
CLI equivalent example:
Scale deployment:
oc scale deployment intro-navigate-git --replicas=3
________________________________________
Step 12: Switch to Administrator Perspective
Now switch perspective.
Steps:
Developer → Administrator
This view shows cluster infrastructure.
Administrator perspective includes:
•	nodes
•	networking
•	operators
•	storage
•	authentication
________________________________________
Step 13: View Project Details
Navigate:
Home → Projects
Select:
intro-navigate
This page shows:
•	project status
•	CPU usage
•	memory usage
•	resource summary
________________________________________
Step 14: View Pods
Menu:
Workloads → Pods
Pods shown for project:
intro-navigate pod
Pod Concept
A pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
Contains:
•	one or more containers
CLI command:
oc get pods
________________________________________
Step 15: View Deployments
Menu:
Workloads → Deployments
You will see:
intro-navigate-git
Click it to view:
•	replica count
•	pod status
•	rollout history
CLI command:
oc get deployments
________________________________________
Step 16: View Services
Menu:
Networking → Services
Service:
intro-navigate-git
Service Purpose
A service provides stable network access to pods.
Because pod IP addresses change.
Service acts as a stable endpoint.
CLI command:
oc get services
________________________________________
Step 17: View Routes
Menu:
Networking → Routes
Route:
intro-navigate-git
Route Purpose
A Route exposes the application externally.
Example URL:
https://app.apps.cluster.com
CLI command:
oc get routes
Create route example:
oc expose service intro-navigate-git
________________________________________
Step 18: Delete the Project
Navigate:
Home → Projects
Select:
Delete Project
Type project name:
intro-navigate
Click:
Delete
CLI equivalent:
oc delete project intro-navigate
________________________________________
Step 19: Logout from Web Console
Click username → Log out
Example:
developer → Log out
________________________________________
Step 20: Login as Admin
Now login using:
username: admin
password: redhatocp
Admin user can view full cluster details.
Important rule:
Do not modify system resources.
________________________________________
Step 21: View Installed Operators
Menu:
Operators → Installed Operators
Operators provide:
•	cluster automation
•	application lifecycle management
Examples:
•	monitoring operator
•	network operator
•	storage operator
________________________________________
Step 22: View All Pods
Menu:
Workloads → Pods
Admin can see pods from all namespaces.
CLI equivalent:
oc get pods -A
________________________________________
Step 23: View Deployments
Menu:
Workloads → Deployments
Displays deployments across the cluster.
CLI command:
oc get deployments -A
________________________________________
Step 24: View Services
Menu:
Networking → Services
Shows all services in the cluster.
CLI command:
oc get services -A
________________________________________
Step 25: Logout Admin
Click:
admin → Log out
________________________________________
Step 26: Finish Lab
Command:
lab finish intro-navigate
Purpose
This command:
•	cleans lab resources
•	resets environment
•	prepares system for next lab
________________________________________
